A||||8||82-T/A||The Fiero IS a great car......||||10||
Z||000000||82-T/A||12-07-2000||05:40 PM||||Just read this from WICKED Fiero..<P><BR>"As you read, you might just start getting depressed like I did at the chances that the Fiero had for greatness. Hope it goes for good reading!"<P>in regards to the SD4, and I wanted to comment on this.<P>In my opinion, the Fiero IS a great car. When any car goes into production, exceptions are made, and it never quite comes out as good as it was intended to.<P>The Fiero never really BOMBED.. so to speak. I mean, they sold hundreds and hundreds of thousands of them. 140 something thousand in the first year. That competes with the number of mustangs sold in 1965!<P>The bottom line is, the Fiero is a perfect chassis. The styling is nearly timeless, and it WILL be a collectible car. You here a few people say bad things about a Fiero.. saying that it's a Firetrap.. and that they flip over (we know how much BS that is)... but those are the same people who turn around and say, "Those Toyota MR2s are death traps!!" Plain and simple, they're ignorant people, and anything short of a Ford Excursion to them is a pathetic car. Different priorities. <P>They are no longer making the Fiero. Thats given, and we have to accept it. BUT... think of your Fiero as a set of Legos. There is soo much you can do with the car. Whether your tastes are conservative, or extreme, there are parts out there for you. Despite popular belief, there are LOTS of aftermarket companies for the Fiero. You can basically completely replace the suspension to the effect that it can outhandle even the newest of Ferraris if you wanted it to. And for a moderately conservative price.<P>You have any number of engine configurations.<P>And despite what people tell you in person, when your driving down the road, and all those heads are turning, the kids in the back of the minivan are pointing, and the girls in the Sunfire's are smiling.. they're NOT thinking about engine fires. I can guarantee you that.<P>Most people don't even know what a Fiero looks like. Most of the people who DO actually know the bad rumors about the Fiero, think that the Fiero looks something like an AMC Gremlin or something.<P>Anyway, I'm bored, and spent the past two days in a horribly boring production meeting. I am the ONLY man in my entire department... I was in a meeting with 14 women.. and then just me.. one man. Every single damn time, the conversation would stray and they'd start talking about maternity leave, or pregnancies, or periods... maybe I should bust out with some comments about urinal pucks.. then see how interested they are.<P>Anyway, just my 2 cents.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||10
Z||000001||DavidHenshaw||12-07-2000||10:57 PM||||Todd, thanks for your comments. I really love my 86 GT purchased 6 months ago and its easy to see why so many of us are so partial to these cars. <P>In a way the limiting of production to 4 years is a good thing. Pontiac wasn't able to mess up the cars basic good looks with only 4 years production. Who knows what a 5th thru 9th year would have looked like. They've butchered other models before.<P>I continue to be amazed at what Pontiac was able to do on a shoestring budget with mid eightees technology.<P>Dave||||reg||1
Z||000002||Black88GT||12-07-2000||11:34 PM||||I love the Fiero too, but I think "perfect chassis" is stretching it...<P>It never really "bombed" but it could hardly be labeled a success by business terms.  The bad reputation is what killed it.  Of course with us it was a great success, but GM didn't think it could lose that reputation so it was in their best interest to kill it, even though the 88s were getting better reviews with the new suspension.<P>I always wonder what would have happened if they started the 84 model year with the 88 version...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||sqoach||12-08-2000||02:08 AM||||correction david, 5 year production. 84, 85, 86, 87, 88.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||WKDFIRO||12-08-2000||04:01 AM||||(Not Flaming ANYONE! Except GM maybe...and BTW I have a BORED meeting coming up myself next week...so I can identify.)<P>When Saturns are touted for their innovations, no mention is ever made about how it was first done by a Fiero. The Fiero is the butt of fire jokes and ignorance. <P>The Fiero was not quite near as good as it was intended. It was no where near it. It was hobbled by bean counters and muddled with bad marketing. It didn't have to be that way is the intent that upsets me. Just a few things that the Fiero had cooking before the plug was pulled:<P>-The 88 suspension design existed in 83. But why spend money on a car selling more than a 100,000 in its first year? Lets all say it together, "Short sighted planning."<BR>-The electro-servo power steering was to be an option in 88. Out goes the Fiero in comes the 89 MR-2, with electro-servo power steering!<BR>-Alcoa, the aluminum manufacturer had designed and ALL ALUMINUM space frame that was lighter and stronger than steel. The prototype had a Quad 4.<BR>-Design concepts were already underway for a factory turbo V6 before 88.<P>The Fiero was expected to sell over a hundred thousand copies EVERY YEAR! (The factory had a top capacity of 120,000 a year) The Mustang does not sell over a hundred thousand a year yet it still exists. The Fiero never sold over a hundred thousand after 84.<P>As long as the ignorance is out there about the Fiero, I don't see how the Fiero can be a collector's car except to their owners. I look to the Corvair (first factory turbo from GM, only rear engined car from GM) which has almost all the unique qualifications to be a "collector" as proof. A book gets written about how it catches fire, all of a sudden no one wants to be near it. It is discontinued even after it is improved every year. It will be a very long time (if ever,IMHO) before 88 Fieros are sold for more than their original sticker price. The very last Fiero ever made is for sale for over $20 grand. It still sits under a cover on blocks in his garage (at least the last time we've heard). An MR-2 with reasonable miles still goes for more than an 84 will ever get. This is good for the person who buys a Fiero to drive and fix up, bad news for investment Fieros. With that said it does create an atmosphere that is unique to the Fiero.<P>What other car discontinued more than 10 years ago, is still evolving? TA has a very good point about this. We are putting the engines in that GM should have. We are improving on the suspension that GM should have, from the beginning. We are the owners who have to put up with the ignorance of having a car that had so much potential of wiping out the Corvette that it had to be stopped. Competition should have improved the breed in GM.<BR>     GM could have made the Fiero a great car that would have toppled the Corvette for the best performance for the money. We are the benefactors of GM's chronic short comings. <BR>     When I get into my Fiero, I dont' get into a firebomb or a mistake. I get into a car that I still feel more comfortable in than any brand new car at the LA Car Show and a car that still puts the fear of Pontiac against the local Hondas.<P>The Fiero is a great car, unfortunately we owners are the only ones who know. But shame on GM for not making the Fiero they should have! <P>(Thats my uhh....42 cents worth...)<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000005||82-T/A||12-08-2000||08:16 AM||||Honestly though, besides a handful of non Fiero owning people.... MOST people out there don't even know what a Fiero looks like.<P>Nearly every person I've had talk to me about my car, hasn't a single clue what kind of car it was. I mean, it says Fiero in the back... I guess they just didn't know to look there. I have people putting little pieces of paper under my windshield asking to buy my car all the time. Little do they know my bearings are shot.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Anyway, all I'm saying is that for any of you, you shouldn't FEEL any of that animosity from people because... I'd say that 95% of the people looking at your car when your driving around are admiring it. They probably think it's some kind of exotic. I mean, don't get me wrong, the Mr2 is neat, but it looks like a Japanese car. Most people wouldn't mistake an MR2 for anything else. Fieros get mistaken for Ferraris on a constant basis. Happens to me quite frequently.<P><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE<BR>http://www.ezshots.com/album.cgi?82tafiero/Apartment||||reg||1
Z||000006||CozmoPooky||12-08-2000||09:19 AM||||For the money, a Fiero is the best bang for the buck available today. There are lots of well-documented flaws and shortcomings in the car, most of which have been solved by aftermarket companies and people like the ones on this forum.  Fieros were almost "factory kit cars", it still amazes me that the General ever let them be built.  All the Fiero community is doing is a natural extension of what the original designers intended. If you look at the vast number of modifications and improvements possible and the low cost involved you can see what a "street rodder's dream" Fieros are.<BR>  These unique cars are going to be around a loong time and will evolve in ways we can't imagine.  Most people that look at my Fiero(s) are pleasantly surprised at how cool the cars really are!   <P>------------------<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||12-08-2000||03:42 PM||||Down on the other side of the continent, in Southern California there are Fieros everywhere. Unfortunately, most of them are 84s that are in pretty sad shape. Having an 85 GT Ive never had anyone ask me what car it was, they only ask me what I got in it. (It rumbles a bit from the cam). In Southern California where you can't spit in the street without hitting a Honda, it can pretty tough. On the streetlight, Ive heard all to often, "Its just a Fiero." (And I don't have the Fiero decal on the back)<P>Just before I suck the doors off of them at least...||||reg||10
Z||000008||PearlBlueSoul||12-08-2000||07:33 PM||||Cars have stereotypes associated with them regardless what type of car it is.  Some things stick, some don't, some really are false, some are way overrated (skyline included) but not everyone gets that.  the games played by society determine what really becomes a best seller and what doesn't, regardless the following the car might have.  The MR2 is no exception.  why do you think no one bought MR2s?  Too small, impracticle, too pricey, too high insurance...all this that and the other.  So, Toyota killed it, and now is bringing it back?  What are they getting from the public?  Same old same old, plus:  It doesn't handle as good as a Miata.  <P>Regardless what cars we own, or have owned in the past, each one of them had their pitfalls when they came out.  Hell, Bugs popped fuel lines and burned to the ground besides Fieros (notice they're VERY common and that fact of the gas blow ups really hasn't stopped the public from restoring/driving them).  The Deuce is no exception either!  Oh no, far from it.  We've had our problems too.  5th gear popout, warping rotors, chinsy light switches, this that and the other, even RUST underneath the trunk mat (I know mine is developing it!).  These things are just part of the car.  i'm sure we're not the first ones to have these problems with our cars, and I'm sure that we'll never hear the end of recalls and bulletins about what flaws cars have.  I said it before and I'll say it again, we take it too for granted that we get cars nearly perfect from the factory.  With all the testing we have now, there's small wonder why we're not tougher on our car makers for more quality in what they build, versus quantity and economy.  <P>I think a segment of the industry doesn't see the car as a work of art, or as any form of passion anymore.  Sometimes not even in the current generation of american sports cars.  As a whole, America has lost it's sense of passion it once had in the 50s-60s, either that or I'm being quite naive.  It seems to me that not many people are really into the whole scene like they used to be.  Cars aren't as flashy, there's no style, everything's the same or built out of a parts bin from sister cars by the same company or same general auto manufacturer.  What's the point building cookie cutter type cars if there's nothing in them but plastic?!  You've got the same answer I'm sure:  none.  There should be more style and finesse put into these newer cars.  I remember my dad rented a focus for a while, and already small trim pieces and pop in plastic fabric keepers were falling into the floorboard!  What gives?!  Can we say:  lack of focus?? (pardon the pun)<P>Sorry for this waste of bandwidth.  Maybe I made sense with someone somewhere.  I just wanted to say that well, you're not alone.  The "other" Mid engine mighty mice feel the pinch too.<BR><P>------------------<BR>1987 Toyota MR2 N/A 5 speed<BR>  <A HREF="http://www.atlantamr2s.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.atlantamr2s.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/darionscard</A>  <BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/darionscard/Thumb.jpg">       <BR><B>Notable mods:</B>  Sus tech springs and sway bars, Toyota strut tower brace, KYB strut inserts, HKS exhaust, NGK plugs/wires, Momo Racer wheels (14x6), Custom intake, Kenwood 12" speaker in front trunk, Jensen amp.||||reg||1
